WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.113 --> 00:00:02.570
For the estimated 25%

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:03.760
of the US workforce

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.930
who have seen their work
shift to be fully remote

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.300
or for those leaving jobs

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.460
in search of more fully remote work,

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.800
there's a new trend that could

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.190
double your salary.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.170
and it's simple,

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.980
work two or sometimes more,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.650
full-time remote jobs
at once and in secret.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:19.590
My household income

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.090
was probably $300,400,000

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.530
somewhere there before,

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:25.970
and now we're over a million.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.210
People think of those numbers

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.350
as really big numbers.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:29.410
It's not possible.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.150
I think it's just a mindset thing.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.030
In a survey published

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.410
on October of 2021,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.430
nearly 40% of remote workers

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.460
admitted to having two full-time jobs.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.660
This movement has been
dubbed over employment,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.570
and an entire community has popped up

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.220
to help navigate their secret double-lives

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.930
with advice like what you can use

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.460
to be on two Zoom calls

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.450
at the same time

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.320
or how you can avoid
burnout by being mediocre.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.230
Proponents believe that for some,

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.410
it can unlock both more
money and less stress

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.730
but for both employers and employees,

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.220
can multiple remote jobs be a sustainable

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.023
part of our future of work?

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.090
Trends in multiple full-time employment

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.730
of course only apply
to certain industries.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:13.869
There are of course some industries

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:16.270
like healthcare and restaurants,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.690
and travel that simply can't be remote,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.240
but with the exception of those,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.550
many have the ability to work from home.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.560
Client facing roles

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.900
or jobs that require in-person attendance

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.540
can't be done simultaneously

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.397
unless they operate in
completely different hours

00:01:29.397 --> 00:01:30.610
of the day.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.630
But that leaves a lot of room

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:32.700
for knowledge workers,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.280
in sectors like technology and finance.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.010
People have gotten a lot more efficient

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.050
working from home.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.880
And so that has reduced the working day

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.330
to some degree and has probably allowed

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:47.150
for especially software
developers for instance,

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.280
to work another job perhaps,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.210
because they've saved on time.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.430
Recent research found that

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:55.140
31% of tech professionals
admit to putting in

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.250
just three to four hours of work a day,

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.320
with one anonymous Facebook
employee saying that

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.140
they put in less than one hour of work

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.600
if you don't factor in
all the hours of meetings.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.433
A lot of people

00:02:04.433 --> 00:02:05.266
in the overemployed community

00:02:05.266 --> 00:02:08.589
are in accounting,
finance, insurance, sales.

00:02:08.589 --> 00:02:10.750
Obviously you know, software developers

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.040
are kind of at the
forefront of the community,

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.420
and still is the most remote friendly job

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.850
just from a supply and demand reason.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.160
This is the voice of Isaac.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.410
He runs the website Overemployed.com

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.320
a site dedicated to give resources,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.960
and promote the idea of working multiple

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.230
full-time jobs.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.700
And being someone who himself

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.330
claims to work two jobs,

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.250
he has asked us to keep his name, voice,

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.680
and image anonymous.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.590
Overemployed really just stemmed

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.700
from me trying,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.520
I guess, it out myself.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:39.353
You know,

00:02:39.353 --> 00:02:40.810
talking through several different lawyers,

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.000
looking at my employment contract,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.410
and seeking knowledge and permission

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:46.243
if you will.

00:02:46.243 --> 00:02:48.240
And then I happened to
talk to a family member

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.232
who just, you know, chuckled at the idea

00:02:50.232 --> 00:02:52.130
'cause she knew many
people that were doing it,

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.525
and it's been the biggest
open secret out there.

00:02:53.525 --> 00:02:55.130
And I was like, okay.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:55.963
For some,

00:02:55.963 --> 00:02:57.410
it may be surprising to
know that what people

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.530
like Isaac are doing

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.200
doesn't necessarily violate any laws.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.290
Generally speaking,

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.010
there's there's no law that would prohibit

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.529
an employee from having two jobs at once.

00:03:06.529 --> 00:03:07.362
At most,

00:03:07.362 --> 00:03:09.830
it can be a violation
of an employer policy.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.070
There are some employers
who have what they call

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.680
no moonlighting policies.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.120
Some are a complete ban
on outside employment.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.139
Some say no outside
employment that interferes

00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:20.770
with your job duties

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.110
or conflicts with what
you do for the employer

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.940
or with the employer's business.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:26.800
Nevertheless,

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:27.980
those who have two jobs

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.810
often go to great lengths
to hide that fact.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.410
I tend to have a very defined structure

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:34.800
on how I structure my days.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.270
My day's actually very busy being a dad.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:38.920
That's my number one priority.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.620
And then I fit work in

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.531
kind of 90 minutes you know sprints,

00:03:42.531 --> 00:03:43.647
I call them,

00:03:43.647 --> 00:03:45.180
for you know, three four of 'em

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.360
throughout the day

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.300
to focus across you know one job

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.760
or two job.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.270
Job one or job two,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.540
and get what I need to get done there.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.130
One thing is never mix the hardware

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:56.060
if you will.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.370
Keep it completely
separated and standalone.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.200
Don't use your employers property.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.590
Their laptops, computers,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.980
printers, paper, whatever
it is to perform work

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.650
for the other employer.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:11.700
And don't ever lie
about not having the job

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.463
because employers can you know,

00:04:13.463 --> 00:04:15.500
fire employees for dishonesty

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.930
no matter what it relates to.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.290
Some companies are catching on

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.570
to potential moonlighters

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.530
using tool is like artificial intelligence

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.570
or productivity monitoring software

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.020
that can track employee engagement

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.470
with various tools like monitoring

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.800
how much your mouse is
moving during the day.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.364
But even for these obstacles,

00:04:30.364 --> 00:04:33.300
the overemployed community
has well, solutions.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.630
And while technology can help

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.420
keep some of these overemployed workers

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.700
from legal issues,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.230
they also need to be aware of their own

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:40.480
mental load.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.920
When you're thinking about two jobs,

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.980
and when you're thinking
about the implications

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.420
of having two jobs,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.010
not just in terms of responsibility

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.020
but in terms of the psychological load

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.970
of having to lie and having to hide,

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.230
I think it, again

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.000
it depends on your unique situation.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.170
It depends on how hard
it is for you to do that,

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.600
especially when we know
that psychologically

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.380
when you feel guilty about something,

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.100
it's like the pressure of being found out,

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.730
can almost be excruciating.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.380
This is Rahaf Harfoush,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:10.500
a digital anthropologist,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.320
and researcher who recently wrote a book

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.410
on hustle culture and burnout.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.220
We're playing all of
this overemployment stuff

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.580
on the backdrop of a global pandemic,

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.320
of opening and closing borders.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.380
We're all already pretty stressed.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.390
Many of us are already pretty stressed

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.130
managing enough uncertainty.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:29.470
So if that's not your personality

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.981
adding yet another giant bundle

00:05:31.981 --> 00:05:35.476
of uncertainty that could
have legal, financial,

00:05:35.476 --> 00:05:37.650
reputational consequences,

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.460
it would just be something
that I would advise

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.814
that a person would really think through

00:05:41.814 --> 00:05:43.860
because you know,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.270
all the money that you could earn

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.530
if it ends up tanking your
reputation in industry

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.200
or having legal consequences,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.270
and stressing you out

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:52.180
so you can't even enjoy your time off.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.140
Those are the hidden costs

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.415
that we need to take a look at.

00:05:54.415 --> 00:05:56.142
I think that is a misconception

00:05:56.142 --> 00:05:57.307
that people have that you know,

00:05:57.307 --> 00:05:59.860
if you do multiple jobs,
you must be overworking.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.940
You don't have to if you're productive,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.490
and thoughtful about how
you allocate your time

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.730
with deep focus work, et cetera

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:07.820
As Isaac explains

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.960
having two jobs, at
least in his experience,

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.440
has actually reduced the
stress around his work.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:14.000
I haven't found it stressful,

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.190
but to each their own.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.190
One of the nice side effects

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.600
of virtual remote work is, you know,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.700
with two jobs or three jobs or whatever,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.040
if you don't like a meeting,

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.090
you don't have to go to it.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:23.923
See what happens.

00:06:23.923 --> 00:06:26.120
Just say client, "Can I
get the meeting notes?"

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.050
You know, if that meeting
was wasting your time,

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.540
it wasn't adding to
your bottom line results

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.520
that you're tasked to produce.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.910
You can state why.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.160
And I think a lot of the overemployers

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.090
are kind of leading some of the charge,

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.070
and pushing back with overworking

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:41.550
that a lot of people with one job

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.240
are over compensating actually,

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:44.073
and overworking

00:06:44.073 --> 00:06:45.580
'cause they worry about their job.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.420
And that's why they're
the ones that are like,

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.200
you know, going through meetings 12 hours

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.560
a day back-to-back.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:51.830
You know, it's a choice.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.070
It's really comes down to,

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:54.783
it's a choice of what
you do with your time.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.350
And taking that time back

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:58.530
is mindset shift

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.498
that is at the core of what the proponents

00:07:00.498 --> 00:07:01.810
of overemployment believe.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.510
Detach yourself from your job.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.720
Don't wrap your identity into work.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:08.090
And strive to simply meet expectations,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.290
not exceed them.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:10.530
In our work culture,

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.500
we have the sociological phenomena called

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.770
work devotion,

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.110
which is a set of behaviors that we all do

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.260
that we've all done

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:19.720
that signal to our
colleagues and coworkers

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:21.560
that we deserve our success.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.300
So what ends up happening is their sense

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.670
of self worth becomes intrinsically linked

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.040
with how hard they work

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.680
with the type of job that they work,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.380
and then it's not even about the job

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:32.213
that they have.

00:07:32.213 --> 00:07:34.140
It's about how hard they're
working for that job.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.130
But we've actually been
seeing research-wise

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.730
economically for years,

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:40.150
is that people are working really hard.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.080
There are some people
that are working two,

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:43.870
and three jobs. Not overemployment,

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.510
just two to three jobs

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.410
in the gig economy, part-time work,

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.970
and still are not able to make ends meet.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:50.890
And so for me,

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:53.330
I think if you are going to do this,

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.440
and separate your sense
of self from your job,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.347
if that's what it takes for you to say,

00:07:57.347 --> 00:07:59.160
"Hey, I am not my job title.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.950
That maybe I wanna be present,

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.950
and joyful and healthy and happy,

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:04.500
and have relationships."

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.660
I think that's great.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:06.770
And even if it is possible

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.090
to work two full-time remote jobs,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.160
it likely isn't gonna be for everyone.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.010
But it does point to a
larger trend happening

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:14.090
in work overall,

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:17.440
a shifting perspective
on our work life balance.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.860
When the pandemic happened,

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.540
and lots of people lost their jobs,

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:22.260
a lot of people were slow to come back

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:23.510
to their employer

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.390
or there was just this psychological shift

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:27.040
where they were rethinking

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.880
the type of work that they wanted,

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:30.720
and that resulted in a labor shortage.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.040
And so now people have
this power to negotiate,

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.270
and sort of get the type
of work that they want.

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:38.430
As the economy recovers,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:40.310
workers have used this new power to find

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:41.840
better working accommodations

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.520
in a movement that some have been calling

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:44.930
the Great Resignation.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.370
We really saw this idea

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:47.500
of the Great Resignation

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.730
start in the middle of last year.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:51.840
And this was as unemployment benefit

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.166
started to kind of unwind,

00:08:53.166 --> 00:08:56.470
and people were going
back to jobs that they had

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:57.580
before the pandemic,

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:59.360
but they realized that the hours

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.250
were very rigorous for example,

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.310
or that the work was not

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:05.750
positioning them for the
future that they wanted.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:08.720
We've moved away from 40 hours a week

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.970
at your desk nine to five,

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.300
and toward people having more autonomy

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.080
over how want to shape what their careers

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.300
look like long-term.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:18.133
And for some

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:18.966
in the overemployed community,

00:09:18.966 --> 00:09:20.940
growing your income
the traditional fashion

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.670
by being promoted can
look less attractive.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.830
For example, according to Glassdoor.com

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:26.930
the average total pay

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.588
for a software engineer is about $156,000

00:09:29.588 --> 00:09:31.050
per year at Google,

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.810
and run $166,000 per year at Meta.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.410
And while Senior Software Engineers

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:37.150
make significantly more money,

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:38.210
it still isn't as much

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.433
as having two more junior roles.

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.070
If you have a full-time job,

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:44.800
you know you are,

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:46.150
you have one source of income.

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:49.580
And if based on continuing
economic disruptions,

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.290
layoffs are becoming more and more common

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.390
across industries.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.650
So does it make sense for you to devote

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.980
all of your time and your extra time

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.320
into one company

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.100
where one day you can
come into the office,

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.280
and suddenly it's like, guess what?

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:02.113
No more job.

00:10:02.113 --> 00:10:04.020
This has just sort of opened a door

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:07.750
for people to navigate
employment in new ways

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:08.900
but it doesn't necessarily mean

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:11.570
people are gonna be able to sustain two,

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.690
three jobs at a time for many years

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.640
because as we mentioned,
burnout is a real thing.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.520
And even if you can do
it for a year or so,

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:20.770
it doesn't mean you can do it you know,

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:21.790
once you've got children

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:24.060
or once you've got other responsibilities

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.120
that kind of take time out of your day.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:27.170
I think you're gonna find

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:28.560
that there are people that are going

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.400
to be engaging in overemployment

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:31.780
for very different reasons

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:32.910
in very different ways.

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:34.640
If there's one thing that we can take away

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:36.190
from overemployment,

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:37.260
it's this idea

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.100
that we have more control over our time

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:40.933
than we think.

00:10:40.933 --> 00:10:43.260
There are things that
we believe to be true

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.210
about working about our day-to-day,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:46.250
about our calendars,

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:47.720
about our relationships with our teams,

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.280
and our bosses that are the way they are

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.050
because we've never questioned them

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.960
because that's just how
they've always been.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:55.650
So the more interesting
thing to think about

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.260
is how can I -

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:58.840
where can I do things a bit differently?

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:00.410
Where can I explore new ideas

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.860
or try new things or
test out new concepts?

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:06.180
And maybe by doing that,
you'll start to shift,

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.970
and realize that your work reality is

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.020
whatever you make it.

